  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA strong surface high around 1035 hPa will slowly \nshift south and east of NY and New England tonight through tomorrow.\nThe surface anticyclone will yield ideal radiational cooling \nconditions with mostly clear/clear skies and light to calm winds for \nchilly lows to fall into the 20s to lower 30s. Our forecast was\na blend of the NBM/MAV guidance. The surface high moves over \nthe Mid Atlantic States on Sunday. The strong subsidence from \nthe high will allow for mostly sunny skies and temps to moderate\nto near to slightly above normal readings to close the weekend.\nPWATS will still run 1 to 2 STDEVs below normal based on the \nlatest NAEFS guidance. Highs will be in the lower to mid 60s in \nthe valleys and 50s to around 60F over the higher terrain. Temps\nwill be slightly milder in the 30s with a few upper 20s on \nSunday night with the mid and upper level flow trending zonal\nwith the mid level ridge axis more surpressed over the MS \nValley. Some patchy valley fog may form with dewpoints rising \nsome and light winds. The approaching cold front will be \nmoisture starved to open the week bringing some clouds with a \nfew light showers for the western Adirondacks. Max temps rise \nabove normal into the mid/upper 50s to mid and spotty upper 60s \nacross the region before the frontal passage. \n\nPost frontal cold advection follows Mon night into Tue with a strong \nCanadian anticylone building in once again with more dry \nweather for Tue through the mid week with temps near to slightly\nbelow normal. A warm front with some clouds and limited \nmoisture drifts through Thu with milder weather to close the \nweek. Another cold front with limited low-level moisture \napproaches on Friday. A few light showers or sprinkles may \nimpact locations north and west of the Capital Region, but most \nlocations will be going on close to two weeks without \nmeasurable rainfall.",
